.TH od "" "Command"
.SH NAME
od \- file dump
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBod [\-bcdox] [\fIfile\fB] [ [+] \fIoffset\fB[.][b] ]\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
od -- file dump
Usage:	od  [ options ]  [ file ]  [ [ + ] offset[.][b] ]
Options:
	-b	Dump bytes in the default base
	-c	Dump bytes as ASCII characters
	-d	Dump words in decimal
	-o	Dump words in octal
	-x	Dump words in hexadecimal
The default base is octal on the PDP-11 and hexadecimal on the i8086.
The offset must be preceded by '+' if 'file' is omitted.
The offset is decimal if '.' is present;
'b' implies 512-byte blocks instead of bytes.
.HE
.B od
prints the specified
.I file
as a sequence of octal or hexadecimal numbers, or machine words.
If no
.I file
is given,
.B od
dumps the standard input.
Only one format option may be given; giving more than one is an error.
In this build (no
.B \-DHEX
compiled in), the default base for
.B \-b
and for machine words when no option is given is octal.
.PP
The following options set the format of
.BR od 's
output:
.IP \fB\-b\fR
Dump bytes in the default base.
.IP \fB\-c\fR
Dump bytes as ASCII characters.
A printable byte is shown as a space and the character; a few control
bytes are shown as a space and a backslash escape (\fB\en\fR,
\fB\et\fR, and similarly for backspace, formfeed, carriage return, and
NUL); any other byte falls back to the default base.
.IP \fB\-d\fR
Dump words in decimal.
.IP \fB\-o\fR
Dump words in octal.
.IP \fB\-x\fR
Dump words in hexadecimal.
.PP
Dumping can start at position
.I offset
into the file.
The
.I offset
is octal unless followed by a
.B .
suffix, which makes it decimal; a trailing
.B b
suffix multiplies it by
.B BUFSIZ
(512-byte blocks) instead of bytes.
An
.I offset
may be given only if a
.I file
is also named, or is preceded by a literal
.B +
if the input is the standard input.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.B conv
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B od
complains and exits if more than one format option is given, if the
named
.I file
cannot be opened, or if the
.I offset
syntax is malformed.
